window.THEOLOGY_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"Did Jesus rise from the dead?","description":"A clear-eyed look at the central claim of Christian faith, the historical questions it raises, and why the answer matters.","content":"A clear-eyed introduction to the historical question, the first witnesses, and the hope Christians find in the empty tomb. “Did Jesus rise from the dead?” is not a small question tucked away at the edge of Christian belief. The earliest Christian writers place it at the center. If Jesus remained dead, the story might still contain wisdom, courage, and tragedy. But it would not be the story Christians mean to tell when they speak of resurrection hope. What do the sources say? The earliest Christian writings we have are letters from Paul, written within a few decades of Jesus’ death. In them, Paul passes on a tradition that Jesus died, was buried, was raised, and appeared to his followers. The Gospels, written later, tell the story in distinct ways: the disciples discover an empty tomb, and people who had followed Jesus encounter him alive. These texts are ancient testimony, not detached modern reports. They were written by people and communities who believed the events mattered. Reading them carefully means attending both to what they claim and to the historical setting in which those claims were made. The sources give us access to early conviction; how we assess that conviction also depends on our wider view of what is possible. History and the limits of a single answer Historians can examine texts, their dates, the development of traditions, and the changes in the lives of the first Christians. Those tools can illuminate why resurrection belief appeared so early and why it reshaped a small movement. Historical method is less able, by itself, to settle whether God acted in a singular way. That question reaches beyond the ordinary patterns historians use to explain events. There is no neutral shortcut around this. Someone who rules out divine action in advance will reach a different conclusion from someone who thinks God can act in history. Naming that difference does not make evidence irrelevant. It helps us be honest about the assumptions we bring to the evidence. Why Christians say it matters For Christians, resurrection is not simply a claim that life continues after death. It is the hope that God vindicated Jesus and began a new creation in which death and violence do not have the final word. That hope is tied to the wounded and embodied life of Jesus, not an escape from the material world. This belief has consequences for the present. If resurrection is true, then the vulnerable are not disposable, grief deserves companionship, and acts of repair are not naïve. Christians do not always live consistently with that hope; the claim still calls communities to practice it. A question worth carrying People arrive at this question from many places: faith, doubt, historical curiosity, grief, or a desire to understand Christian neighbors. A thoughtful exploration need not rush anyone to a conclusion. It can begin by reading the earliest accounts, asking what they actually say, and noticing what assumptions shape our response. The resurrection is both a historical claim and a theological confession. To ask whether Jesus rose is also to ask what kind of world we inhabit, what we believe God can do, and whether hope can be more than wishful thinking. Continue exploring: read our reflection on the Psalms , or browse more theology articles .","category":"Theology","author":"Anthony William","type":"Article","url":"articles/theology/did-jesus-rise-from-the-dead/index.html","date":"2026-10-15"},{"title":"Who wrote the Gospels?","description":"A beginner-friendly guide to the four Gospel accounts, their voices, and how they came to be read together.","content":"Matthew, Mark, Luke, and John are not four interchangeable reports. Each gives a portrait of Jesus shaped by memory, testimony, and the needs of a community learning to follow him. Four voices, one good news The books bear the names of Matthew, Mark, Luke, and John. Early Christian tradition connected them with people close to the apostolic generation: Matthew and John among the disciples, Mark as a companion of Peter, and Luke as a careful investigator associated with Paul. Modern readers continue to discuss how the texts were composed and transmitted. The Gospels themselves do not read like modern biographies with author biographies attached. They are carefully shaped testimonies, written to proclaim what God has done through Jesus. Read each account on its own terms Mark moves with urgency; Matthew places Jesus within Israel's scriptures; Luke emphasizes the breadth of God's welcome; John invites readers into a reflective, symbolic witness. Their differences are not a problem to erase. They reward patient attention. Read one Gospel from beginning to end. Notice what it repeats, what it leaves room to wonder about, and how its portrait of Jesus asks something of you. Then read another. The voices are distinct, and together they offer a richer view. Continue exploring the Psalms as a school for prayer .","category":"Scripture","author":"Anthony William","type":"Article","url":"articles/scripture/who-wrote-the-gospels/index.html","date":"2026-10-14"},{"title":"What is grace?","description":"Grace is more than a religious word for kindness. It names a gift that changes how we receive life and one another.","content":"Grace is the name Christians give to God's unearned generosity: a welcome we do not manufacture and a life we cannot claim as our achievement. A gift before a reward We often imagine that acceptance comes after we have proved ourselves. Grace begins somewhere else. It says that love is not a wage paid for flawless performance. We are met in our need and invited into a relationship that can remake us. This does not make our choices irrelevant. Rather, grace gives us a place from which to change without pretending we can earn our way into belonging. Gratitude, not fear, becomes the ground of a faithful life. Grace received and shared To receive grace is also to learn its shape. We become more patient with other people, more ready to repair harm, and less eager to treat anyone as disposable. Grace is not permission to ignore injustice; it is the power to seek healing without giving up on one another. Christian communities are at their best when their welcome is tangible: when people can tell the truth, find help, and begin again. That is grace made visible in ordinary life. Explore more theology articles or consider the resurrection .","category":"Theology","author":"Anthony William","type":"Article","url":"articles/theology/what-is-grace/index.html","date":"2026-10-13"},{"title":"Why do Christians pray?","description":"Prayer is not a way to control outcomes; it is a practice of attention, honesty, and relationship.","content":"Christians pray because faith is a relationship, not simply a set of ideas. Prayer makes room to speak, listen, lament, give thanks, and bring the whole of life before God. Prayer is not a technique It is easy to treat prayer like a transaction: say the right words and receive the result we want. Christian prayer certainly includes asking for help, but it does not promise control. It teaches us to be honest about desire while remaining open to wisdom we cannot yet see. Jesus' own prayers include joy, grief, gratitude, and anguish. The Psalms do too. This range reminds us that prayer does not require polished feelings. We can begin with what is true. A practice of attention Prayer can be as simple as a few quiet minutes, a familiar prayer repeated slowly, or a conversation with God during an ordinary task. Its purpose is not to escape the world but to become more attentive within it: to notice need, receive mercy, and respond with love. Over time, prayer forms a person and a community. It loosens the grip of self-sufficiency and helps us carry other people in compassion. The practice may feel small, but faithfulness often grows through small, repeated acts. Try praying with the Psalms as a next step.","category":"Spiritual formation","author":"Anthony William","type":"Article","url":"articles/spiritual-formation/why-do-christians-pray/index.html","date":"2026-10-12"},{"title":"The quiet work of becoming a neighbor","description":"What if the good life is less about finding ourselves and more about learning to be present to one another?","content":"We often speak of a good life as a project of discovery: find your purpose, uncover your gifts, become more fully yourself. But the language of discovery can leave out an essential question: who is my life for? In the stories of scripture, love is rarely an abstract feeling. It takes the shape of attention. A traveler stops. A household makes room. Someone listens long enough to hear what another person is actually saying. The neighbor is not an idea to admire from a distance, but a person whose presence interrupts our plans. That interruption can feel inconvenient. We are trained to measure a day by what we finish, and attention can look unproductive. Yet the small acts of staying, remembering a name, sharing a meal, or asking one more question are not detours from a faithful life. They are often where that life becomes visible. Becoming a neighbor is not a grand achievement to add to a personal résumé. It is a practice of receiving the people already near us, and allowing their needs and joys to matter. We may not solve every problem. We can begin by refusing to treat anyone as background. A question to carry with you: who has been near you lately, and what might it look like to give them your unhurried attention?","category":"Spiritual formation","author":"Anthony William","type":"Article","url":"articles/spiritual-formation/the-quiet-work-of-becoming-a-neighbor/index.html","date":"2026-10-11"},{"title":"What does loving your neighbor mean today?","description":"Turning a familiar command into attentive, practical care for the people around us.","content":"“Love your neighbor” is familiar enough to become abstract. The question is how a sweeping command becomes a concrete way of seeing and serving the people who share our days. Begin with attention A neighbor is not only an idea or a cause. It is the person whose name we can learn, the colleague who needs to be heard, the family nearby carrying a burden we have not noticed. Attention is a first act of love because it lets another person become real to us. Jesus' story of the Good Samaritan unsettles the boundaries we use to decide who deserves our care. The neighbor is not simply the person most like us. We become neighbors when we move toward need with compassion. Make care practical Love may mean sharing a meal, keeping a promise, offering a ride, speaking up when someone is treated unjustly, or making room for a difficult conversation. These actions rarely look dramatic. Their faithfulness is part of their importance. We will not meet every need, and love does not require pretending that we can. It asks us to respond honestly to the people and responsibilities entrusted to us, and to build communities where care can be shared. Read also how to disagree without becoming enemies .","category":"Faith & culture","author":"Anthony William","type":"Article","url":"articles/faith-and-culture/loving-your-neighbor-today/index.html","date":"2026-10-10"},{"title":"What is Christian faith?","description":"Faith includes belief, but also trust, practice, and a life shaped in relationship with God and others.","content":"Christian faith is often reduced either to agreeing with a list of claims or to having a certain feeling. In practice, it brings together what we trust, what we hope, and how we live. Belief and trust belong together Belief matters. Christians make claims about God, Jesus, creation, forgiveness, and the hope of renewal. Yet faith is not only intellectual agreement. To have faith is also to entrust oneself to the God those claims describe, especially when certainty feels incomplete. The biblical language of faith often carries this sense of reliance and faithfulness. It is less like solving a puzzle once and more like learning to walk a path with others. A life shaped by hope Faith becomes visible in practice: worship, prayer, care for neighbors, truth-telling, generosity, and the work of reconciliation. These practices do not purchase God's love. They are ways of responding to love already received. Faith can include questions and seasons of struggle. A community of faith should make room for honest seeking, because trust grows through relationship, patience, and shared life. Consider grace as the gift at the heart of faith .","category":"Theology","author":"Anthony William","type":"Article","url":"articles/theology/what-is-christian-faith/index.html","date":"2026-10-09"},{"title":"Reading the Psalms as a school for prayer","description":"The ancient practice of giving our whole selves a language before God.","content":"Some days we arrive at prayer with clear words. Other days, we bring a tangle of gratitude, grief, impatience, and hope, and do not know where to begin. The Psalms meet us in that honest place. They do not ask us to tidy our feelings before we speak. Across these poems, praise sits beside complaint, trust alongside bewilderment. The psalmist asks hard questions and remembers goodness. This range is a gift: prayer can hold the truth of our experience without pretending that every season feels the same. Reading a psalm slowly can become a way of learning its language. Pause at a phrase that catches your attention. Repeat it. Notice what it gives voice to, or where you resist it. Over time, this practice can widen our prayers. We learn words for joy and lament, words for our own lives and words that make room for the suffering of others. We need not manufacture eloquence. We can borrow an old song and make it our own. Try this: read one psalm aloud, then sit quietly with a single line. What does it help you say today?","category":"Scripture","author":"Anthony William","type":"Article","url":"articles/scripture/reading-the-psalms-as-a-school-for-prayer/index.html","date":"2026-10-07"},{"title":"Can we disagree without becoming enemies?","description":"Recovering the patient art of listening in a loud and divided age.","content":"Disagreement is not a rare interruption in community. It is one of the ways we discover that our neighbors are real people, with histories and convictions that cannot be reduced to our own. The question is not whether differences will arise, but what we will do when they do. Listening does not require us to surrender conviction or pretend that every position is equally wise. It asks something more demanding: that we understand what another person means before we decide how to answer. Curiosity is not agreement. It is a way of treating a person as more than the view we disagree with. That kind of attention takes practice. We can ask what experience shaped a belief, reflect back what we heard, and make room for a pause before responding. We can also name harm clearly and set boundaries when a conversation is unsafe. Reconciliation is not the same as avoiding truth. Communities can choose to tell the truth without contempt, to repair where trust has been broken, and to keep seeking the good of one another. A question to carry with you: what would change if your next disagreement began with a sincere effort to understand?","category":"Faith & culture","author":"Anthony William","type":"Article","url":"articles/faith-and-culture/can-we-disagree-without-becoming-enemies/index.html","date":"2026-10-04"},{"title":"Ordinary time, holy life","description":"Finding the sacred not outside our routines, but within their steady rhythms.","content":"Much of life does not feel remarkable while we are living it. We make breakfast, answer messages, walk familiar streets, and return to the people and tasks that need us. It is tempting to imagine that a more spiritual life waits somewhere beyond these ordinary hours. But the practices that sustain us are often made of repetition. A shared meal, a quiet moment before the day begins, a weekly gathering, a familiar prayer: each gives our attention a shape. The ordinary is not automatically holy, but it can become a place where we learn patience, gratitude, and care. We do not have to turn every routine into a performance of devotion. It may be enough to notice where life is already being given: food prepared and shared, work offered with care, rest accepted without apology. Perhaps holiness is not always a dramatic departure from everyday life. Perhaps it is also a steady return to it, with eyes open to the people and gifts already here. Try this: choose one ordinary routine today and give it your full attention for a few minutes.","category":"Spiritual formation","author":"Anthony William","type":"Article","url":"articles/spiritual-formation/ordinary-time-holy-life/index.html","date":"2026-10-01"},{"title":"Confessions","description":"A foundational spiritual autobiography shaped as a long prayer. Augustine reflects on memory, desire, friendship, and the search for God.","content":"data-category=\"theology\" data-search=\"confessions augustine hippo classic spiritual autobiography prayer\" BOOK · CLASSIC Confessions Augustine of Hippo A foundational spiritual autobiography shaped as a long prayer. Augustine reflects on memory, desire, friendship, and the search for God.","category":"Library","author":"Augustine of Hippo","type":"BOOK · CLASSIC","url":"library.html#confessions"},{"title":"Life Together","description":"A compact reflection on Christian community, shared practices, and the difference between loving an ideal and loving actual people.","content":"data-category=\"community\" data-search=\"life together dietrich bonhoeffer community church discipleship\" BOOK · COMMUNITY Life Together Dietrich Bonhoeffer A compact reflection on Christian community, shared practices, and the difference between loving an ideal and loving actual people.","category":"Library","author":"Dietrich Bonhoeffer","type":"BOOK · COMMUNITY","url":"library.html#life-together"},{"title":"The Cross and the Lynching Tree","description":"A significant work of theology bringing the Christian story of the cross into conversation with the history and ongoing reality of racial violence in the United States.","content":"data-category=\"theology\" data-search=\"the cross and the lynching tree james h cone theology justice race\" BOOK · THEOLOGY The Cross and the Lynching Tree James H. Cone A significant work of theology bringing the Christian story of the cross into conversation with the history and ongoing reality of racial violence in the United States.","category":"Library","author":"James H. Cone","type":"BOOK · THEOLOGY","url":"library.html#cross-and-lynching-tree"},{"title":"A beginner's guide to reading the Bible","description":"A starting framework for reading biblical texts with attention to genre, historical context, literary shape, and the communities who have read them.","content":"data-category=\"scripture\" data-search=\"beginner guide reading bible scripture study reading guide\" READING GUIDE · SCRIPTURE A beginner's guide to reading the Bible Anthony William A starting framework for reading biblical texts with attention to genre, historical context, literary shape, and the communities who have read them.","category":"Library","author":"Anthony William","type":"READING GUIDE · SCRIPTURE","url":"library.html#bible-guide"}];
